'm sorry I can't give you a seven  minutes answer for how long you wait  until you switch but things to look for  are remember how it would look like this  but then you'd get puffs of the yellow  smoke and so you want to wait and see  you're no longer getting those puffs of  yellow smoke because those are areas  that haven't gotten any heat yet that  are now starting to catch on fire and  then later on what queued me to cover it  up was we're starting to get black smoke  and the black smoke tends to mean that  we're we're burning everything really  fast and so that's what we needed to  cover it up but it is a judgment call  and it is tricky and so there's no easy  way  everything from humidity to the  temperature to the type of the the way  that what was in the drum before that's  another thing to think about is if when  you get an oil drum you don't know where  you're getting it from and so it may  have oil in it and so you may need to do  like a pre burn with just a little bit  of material to burn out whatever junk is  in the drum so that you have a clean  drum clean-ish drum to use for  carbonization because you didn't you  wouldn't want like oil and pregnant for  cats because that would burn pretty  nastily and you can do side by side  carbonization if you have a few drums  which is nice and then covering it up  just doing it relatively quickly but  also carefully so you don't get burned  because the drum is really hot at that  point for making the binder just  remembering that you're going for this  really it's not like consistency so  checking to make sure you get it before  you use it because if you don't the  briquettes will completely fall apart  and that's problematic and then using  the right amount so that you actually  get a good briquette is important and  then briquetting so I just wanted to  show you a couple of other briquette  press examples so the press presses we  used yesterday are really simple and  I'll talk about how we got there but  there's a cup an ejector pin and a  plunger and the block is just because we  don't have tables with holes in them  around here but it's pretty easy to find  some sort of hole you can use so you  don't actually need a block but you  should every team should plan to bring a  press with you so and then ideally you  can make them there but it's nice to  have one it's a show as an example to  your welder you can leave it there if  you need to and Haiti our colleagues got  tired of making one briquette at a time  so they came up with this press that  makes four at a time and then has a  plate that slides out sort of like our  old-style press that I'll show you in a  minute to make four briquettes at a time  the problem is you're just increasing  the area so you're distributing the  pressure and so you really have to wail  on this press to make briquettes well  and 2 mm 9 they made an  device to make briquettes which is nice  but a lot of material so yeah and just  when you briquette them make sure you're  hammering with enough force that you  actually get well compacted briquettes  or else they won't when they dry they'll  fall apart so I want to talk a little  bit about how the briquette press came  to be because I think a lot of d-lab you  sort of you work on a project and then  you're done you hand off the community  maybe and it seems like that's all that  it takes and at least in this case and I  think in a lot of cases there's more  required to get to a good product so  initially we made briquettes just by  hand as you can see in this picture and  you can imagine compressing that  material hard enough to get a really  solid briquette it was pretty painful on  the hands and it really didn't make very  good briquettes even if you were super  strong compressing the briquettes so  then we looked at buying a briquette  maker and the mechanised ones that we  could find were about eight thousand  dollars and it's entirely inappropriate  and so there was a bunch of work done by  