e thick and this is a really  expensive piece of metal just right here  and so we were pretty concerned about  the prices of some of these elements and  so we had another person who does a lot  of work in Guatemala take a look at it  and redesign it unfortunately I wasn't  able to find one of these presses in  d-lab today but he just made some subtle  improvements so that that big plate was  cut down until it was just as wide as  the diameter of this shaft and then  everything else was made with an angle  iron an angle iron is relatively cheap  and available everywhere and so that  made it a whole lot more affordable for  our partners another yeah there a  variety of changes and so you can see  just just looking at it there there's a  bit loose metal here is the biggest  change and that improved things a lot  there is it that was not I would say  that very significant compared to  actually this method I think that the  meant that we have now is there's more  waste that we just do it on the tarp so  that we collect that waste so we still  weren't happy with the price and so we  ended up radically redesigning it by  removing all the material basically to  just the most basic elements and so and  we started with a circular design and  that still stands but we've also added a  square design so that if you have a  cylindrical stock you can use it and if  you don't if you have a square stock you  can use it one of the nice things about  square stock is that it's a little bit  more flexible in terms of these parts  the two parts that you need to fit into  the into the press you don't need to cut  a careful circle you can just cut a  square and that's a lot easier and then  same on the bottom you can just weld  these pieces on so we took that to the  field and we got a few different designs  back as improvements so this one is just  just used sheet metal that's bent around  and then welded on the edges and so that  further reduced cost because it's  thinner it's a thick sheet but not as  thick and then this group used angle  iron you can see and put four pieces of  angle to iron together and weld of the  edges which is requires a talented  welder but if you have one does is a  really nice way to do things and so  there's a whole variety of little  improvements that are still being made  but generally we're very happy with this  press now it's interesting a note that  some of our partners still prefer this  press as I mentioned because of the  whole bigger-is-better issue that they  just really like this because they feel  like it's sturdier even though it's ten  times the cost and has no additional  value in terms of speed or labor comfort  or anything like that so that's been one  of our challenges as to people who saw  this press first they still want to use  it because they prefer it  despite the cost difference and so  that's one thing we're trying to work  out is how do we get that cachet into  this press that is so much more  affordable so all that des said and this  is a lot of years of effort of improving  and approving in small and big ways and  so it's just worth thinking about all  the different ways that a product needs  to be improved past the you know initial  prototype which is the stage that you'll  be getting to so I wanted to make a  couple other comments about running a  training because you guys will be doing  at least two group two of the three  groups will be doing the charcoal  trainings in the field so just to start  out with you guys have any questions  about how you'll be running that  training have you thought about it at  all yet how are you feeling about doing  that yeah yeah so your mentor your trip  neither has the contact for your  community partner and so you guys should  be in contact with them to find out what  is there and what needs to be brought  and you should also be reminding them  right now that they need raw material  the the waste they should be gathering  that and drying it right now any ot